import{_ as a,o as t,c as i,a3 as r}from"./chunks/framework.1Y430-yj.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Research on Theory and Practice of Complex Serial/Parallel Mixed Production","description":"","frontmatter":{"lang":"en-US","translationSource":{"title":"复杂串并行混产理论与实践研究","archivedAt":"2026-10-23T14:20:34.857Z","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=NHHXhzWI123bEmz73RliaL","scope":"page"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"logistics/src/page-NHHXhzWI123bEmz73RliaL.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-NHHXhzWI123bEmz73RliaL.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"logistics/src/page-NHHXhzWI123bEmz73RliaL.md"};function n(s,e,c,l,p,h){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="复杂串并行混产理论与实践研究" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="theory-and-practice-of-complex-serial-parallel-mixed-production" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="research-on-theory-and-practice-of-complex-serial-parallel-mixed-production" tabindex="-1">Research on Theory and Practice of Complex Serial/Parallel Mixed Production <a class="header-anchor" href="#research-on-theory-and-practice-of-complex-serial-parallel-mixed-production" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Research on Theory and Practice of Complex Serial/Parallel Mixed Production&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>NHHXhzWI123bEmz73RliaL</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=NHHXhzWI123bEmz73RliaL" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p><a href="./page-zfM2Zf9DLRvNytZWjXcqpG">Single-Pyro-Furnace Heavy Xiranite Plan Based on Clock Signals</a></p><p><a href="./page-6cxYurCEQwPdrwswvpgXjr">Automatic Switching for Reactions with Multiple Raw Materials</a></p><p><span id="source-block-zXp8jky1KBHbdiXWYJEgbb"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="基本概念" aria-hidden="true"></span><h4 id="basic-concepts" tabindex="-1">Basic Concepts <a class="header-anchor" href="#basic-concepts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Basic Concepts&quot;">​</a></h4><p><strong>Mixed production:</strong> One machine executes multiple recipes in different time slots and input slots, saving power by avoiding the need for multiple machines.</p><p><strong>Serial production:</strong> One machine executes different recipes in turn, so machines assigned fractional throughput do not waste their remaining capacity.</p><p><strong>Parallel production:</strong> A reaction tank executes different recipes at the same time, allowing recipes for multiple reaction tanks to run simultaneously and reducing the number of reaction tanks required.</p><p><span id="source-block-4A93oOQD9zx5Rpb882sG3p"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="自动清灰" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="automatic-residue-clearing" aria-hidden="true"></span><h4 id="automatic-clearing" tabindex="-1">Automatic Clearing <a class="header-anchor" href="#automatic-clearing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Automatic Clearing&quot;">​</a></h4><p>In serial machine operation, different recipes may require different quantities of raw materials. Suppose recipe 2a→A and 2b→B are mixed through a single input. When the input sequence is abbbb..., the machine may not contain enough of either recipe&#39;s materials. The next input also differs from what is already inside the machine and cannot enter, so the machine locks up. Traditionally, the player must remove the item manually so that the subsequent b inputs can proceed; this removal of a is called clearing.</p><p>To avoid this, inputs can be mixed in a specific ratio from the start, such as aabbaabb.... For some recipes, however, that mixture is too complicated and may also be affected by phase changes when logging in or out, so this approach is often impractical.</p><p>Instead of removing a manually, another a can be supplied so that it becomes A and naturally leaves the machine. This is the core idea of automatic clearing.</p><p>Feed the machine a raw material that would otherwise lock it up, letting the reaction consume it and release the other material. Clearly, if there is too much of the locking material a, it is effectively being used entirely to produce A, defeating the purpose of serial production. The frequency of this extra input must therefore be limited: input it only occasionally, allowing the a reaction to finish and the machine to switch naturally to b. At that point, the plentiful b material further prevents extra a from entering and leaves it waiting at the input.</p><p><span class="mark-grey">言: In other words: slow down a while leaving b at full speed to switch to b production, and do the reverse to switch to a production.</span></p><p>After the core idea comes the implementation detail. Which materials need extra input? They are the materials that differ between the two recipes being switched and whose recipe quantity is greater than 1. Materials that do not need extra input can be supplied as quickly as possible, provided they do not occupy an input required for automatic clearing. Automatic clearing appears to reduce throughput severely because each clearing waits for an unknown duration of extra input. To solve this, switch only when one of the materials requiring extra input is nearly exhausted, minimizing the number of switches. Extra inputs cannot be mixed with other inputs.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=a(o,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
